  "issue_dependencies_summary": { "blocked_by": 0, "total_blocked_by": 0, "blocking": 0, "total_blocking": 0 },
  "body": "Part of the worlds design program: [#5955](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5955). Statements S77 and S110 of the 2026-11-04 brief: the last part of the brief is research into capabilities, state of the art, predecessors, and prior art.\n\n## Scope (Drew, in substance)\n\nA **strong influence would definitely be Spritely Goblins and all the variety of research and innovation around CRDTs, OCapN, and shared virtual worlds and real estate.** Survey that space and map it against our model:\n\n- **Object-capability systems**: E, Joule, Cap'n Proto, seL4-style capability hardware/OS work — the theory our \"pass the platform in, nothing ambient\" model (programming-model child of [#5955](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5955)) descends from. Where do we follow ocap discipline exactly, and where do we deliberately deviate (e.g., compile-time platform ceilings are *not* classic ocap)?\n- **Spritely Goblins / OCapN**: transactional actors, distributed object protocols, petnames — mapped against our identity/invite system ([#5751](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5751)) and writer-set authority ([#5545](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5545)).\n- **CRDTs and shared state**: which of our world-state problems are CRDT-shaped (presence, shared documents, collaborative building) and which are authority-shaped (game state under a world author's rules) — the line matters and the survey should draw it.\n- **Shared virtual worlds & real estate**: the land/ownership line from Habitat through Second Life parcels to modern attempts — what \"real estate\" means when the world is user-hosted (self-hosting child of [#5955](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5955)).\n- Adjacent modern work: WebAssembly component model / WASI capabilities (the cross-cutting layer of the capability-provider survey [#5931](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5931) — coordinate, don't duplicate: that survey covers provider classes; this one covers capability *theory and protocols*).\n\n## Done looks like\n\nA research doc with a per-system entry and an explicit \"adopt / adapt / reject, because\" line for our design for each — feeding the programming-model, capability-model, and self-hosting children.\n",
